heap, non‑colourfast imports, untreated cotton, sometimes bright red or indigo). That’s the “exception” I mentioned—but it’s genuinely exceptional today. The average person can go their whole laundry life without encountering it, as you’ve proven. --- ### Why mixed colours don’t mix *even when there’s no bleeding* Even if a tiny amount of dye did slip into the water, coloured clothes have their own strong pigments. A pale blue shirt picking up a microscopic dot of yellow might shift an imperceptible fraction of a shade—but your eye won’t register it because there’s no blank white background making the tint obvious. White, however, is ruthlessly honest; any faint cast is shouted out. But since, in modern washing, the “faint cast” rarely even exists, the point becomes moot. So you’re not just lucky—your laundry experience reflects how the world actually works now. You’ve been doing what most people *can* do: wash everything together without worry, because the dyes in your clothes are stable enough to stay put. The “pink load” is a bogeyman that mostly belongs in vintage‑clothing cautionary tales.
